{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And to Moses, etc.</b> The straightforward sense of the verses indicates that this passage was stated in its proper place after the giving of the Torah, and there is no need to elaborate on the various lines of proof that testify to this matter. And likewise I saw that Rabbenu Avraham ibn Ezra, Rashbam, and Ramban were all unanimously of this opinion." "<b>Except</b> that I saw in the Mekhilta a view that is undisputed, namely that on the fifth day of the month there was an interlude in which Moses built the altar mentioned in the portion of Mishpatim, and he forged a covenant with them concerning several commandments that they had already accepted upon themselves — according to one opinion, concerning seven commandments, and according to another, concerning what they had been commanded at Marah. And there are among our teachers who said there that it was concerning the commandments stated in the portion of Behar Sinai through the end of Im BeChukotai. And the implication of his words is that these preceded before the giving of the Torah, and concerning them the aforementioned covenant was forged, because no covenant had been forged until now for any acceptance of a commandment, and Moses tested them whether of their own accord they would uphold the covenant concerning what had already passed, even though HaShem had not obligated them for it. Or perhaps, because Moses spoke thus in the sense that HaShem hinted to him by saying (Exodus 19:3) ״וְתַגִּיד לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — and our Sages of blessed memory interpreted (Shabbat 87a) that the intention was to tell him: draw them with words of Aggadah, and this too is part of drawing the heart, that of their own accord they would swear concerning the earlier ones. Also, from the passage in the Gemara (Shabbat 88a) where they said that Israel preceded Na'aseh to Nishma, when Moses came to them and sixty myriads of angels descended and bound for them two crowns, etc. — this indicates that before the giving of the Torah this passage had already occurred."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {1 "The lemma אֶלָּא (literally 'except/but') continues directly from the previous comment's citation of Ramban et al.; translated as 'Except' to preserve the connective."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:33:13.936201876Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}